    "note": "Human hemoglobin from one study, both at 1.25 Angstrom by X-ray diffraction: deoxy (T state, entry 2DN2) and oxy (R state, entry 2DN1). The coordinates are the prepared composite hemoglobin-tr-figure, built from its recipe. Every atom is a deposited atom of 2DN2 or 2DN1 moved by one rigid-body transform. The 2DN1 dimer and its assembly-operator copy are fitted on the T alpha 1 beta 1 pair, as in the motion stage. Both tetramers are then turned together so that the axis of the T-to-R turn of alpha 2 beta 2 (14.1 degrees, 1.3 Angstrom along it) points 35 degrees away from the line of sight, and set 95 Angstrom apart. The outline behind R is the T alpha 2 beta 2 pair. Waters and the two toluene molecules of 2DN1 are left out."
  },
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "figure",
      "title": "T and R side by side",
      "caption": "Left: deoxy hemoglobin, the T state (2DN2). Right: oxy hemoglobin, the R state (2DN1), with O2 on every heme. Both are drawn at one scale with the green alpha 1 beta 1 pair in the same pose, seen from the alpha 2 beta 2 side, 35 degrees off the axis about which the blue alpha 2 beta 2 pair turns 14 degrees from T to R. The grey outline behind R is where alpha 2 beta 2 sits in T. Beta is the lighter shade. Red: heme. Gold: iron. White: O2. Orange: the contact that changes. T holds alpha 1 Tyr 42 to beta 2 Asp 99 at 2.5 \u00c5; R lets it go and holds alpha 1 Asp 94 to beta 2 Asn 102 at 2.8 \u00c5.",
